remote: how winch reaches a host and runs there

winch works on a host through one SSH connection, the session, with a
helper program at the other end.

What a host needs:
  - Debian 13, Ubuntu 24.04, or Ubuntu 26.04, on amd64 or arm64.
    Another architecture fails the host.
  - Login as root, or as a user with passwordless sudo. winch runs
    sudo -n. When sudo fails, the error shows the message of sudo.
  - A POSIX shell as the login shell. It prints nothing on stdout
    before the command of winch starts.
  - sh, id, uname, head, ls, and sha256sum.
  - /var/lib/winch on a file system that allows running programs, not
    one mounted noexec. When /var/lib/winch or its bin/ exists, root
    owns it, and others cannot write to it.

  Nothing else needs to be installed. The machine that runs winch is
  Linux or macOS with the OpenSSH client.

SSH settings:
  winch runs your ssh, so ~/.ssh/config applies: Port, IdentityFile,
  ProxyJump, and every other setting. Its Host blocks match the host
  name of the inventory, also when the host has an address. The
  address and the user of the inventory override the SSH config.
  --user overrides both.

    # ~/.ssh/config
    Host *.acme.com
      Port 2222
      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519

    # inventories/production.yaml
    hosts:
      web1.acme.com:
        address: 192.0.2.10
        user: admin

    # winch logs in to 192.0.2.10 on port 2222 as admin,
    # with the key ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519.

  winch also passes these options. They win over the SSH config:

    ConnectTimeout=15        a host that does not answer fails
    ServerAliveInterval=15   and does not hang the run
    ServerAliveCountMax=3
    RemoteCommand=none       the SSH config does not replace
    PermitLocalCommand=no    or add to the command of winch
    ClearAllForwardings=yes
    ForwardAgent=no
    ForwardX11=no

Host keys and passphrases:
  When stdin and stderr are terminals, ssh can ask for a new host key
  or a key passphrase. Sessions then start one at a time, in inventory
  order, so each question belongs to one host. After the start, hosts
  run in parallel.

  Without a terminal, or with --batch, winch passes BatchMode=yes and
  sessions start in parallel. A host that needs a question is then
  unreachable. Accept the host key and load the key into ssh-agent
  first:

    ssh -o HostName=192.0.2.10 -l admin web1.acme.com
    ssh-add ~/.ssh/acme_ed25519
    winch apply site --batch

    # ssh asks no questions; the apply question stays.

  The access checks and the connections after a reboot always use
  BatchMode=yes. Run winch doc workflow for the access checks.

When a connection fails:
  A host is unreachable when ssh fails before the session starts: the
  name does not resolve, the connection is refused or takes more than
  15 seconds, or the host key or the login fails. winch then exits 4
  when no step failed. For an unknown host key, the error names the ssh
  command to run once. For a refused login, it names ssh-add.

  When the host closes the connection before the SSH handshake, as a
  busy sshd does at MaxStartups, winch tries once more after 2
  seconds. Every other failure fails the host at once, so a retry
  never counts as a second failed login.

  These fail the host, but it is not unreachable:
  - sudo needs a password, or the user may not use sudo.
  - Another winch run holds the lock of the host. The error names
    that run and the process ID of its helper. When the run is gone,
    end that process.
  - The session breaks after the start.

The session:
  A session is one ssh process with the helper at the other end. All
  work on a host goes through it. It opens at the first work of the
  host and stays open across stages. It closes when the host has no
  more work, and at the apply question, which can wait for a long
  time. The access checks open their own connections.

  At the start, a short script looks for the helper in
  /var/lib/winch/bin/ by its SHA-256 hash. When the helper is missing,
  winch sends it through the same session. The helper takes a lock, so
  one host has one session at a time. It exits when the session ends
  and leaves no process, except a confirm_access timer until it ends.

  The helpers for amd64 and arm64 are inside the winch binary. A winch
  built with go install has none: plan, apply, run, and exec fail
  before they contact a host. Install a release.

How commands run:
  Every command on the host, also one that a module runs, such as
  apt-get or systemctl, runs as root in /, with this environment:

    LC_ALL  C.UTF-8
    PATH    /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    HOME    /root
    umask   022

  LANGUAGE and the variables that sudo adds are not set. The commands
  of cmd, assert, unless, validate, then, health, and the certbot hooks
  run through sh -e, with stdin from /dev/null. A command of several
  lines stops at its first failed line.

  A cmd with dir runs in that directory. A cmd with user runs as that
  user, with the home of that user as HOME. It starts no login
  session: PAM does not run, so the login umask and limits of the user
  do not apply.

The time limit:
  A command stops after 10 minutes, or after the timeout of a cmd
  step in seconds. The helper sends SIGTERM to the command and its
  child processes, and SIGKILL 10 seconds later to those that still
  run. The step fails, and the error shows the last 10 lines of
  output. apt-get in pkg and upgrade has no time limit. A command that
  exits while a child process keeps its output open succeeds.

  When the connection to winch ends, the helper lets the running
  command finish within its limit, and then exits. A package install
  is not cut off in the middle. winch exec is different: on Ctrl-C, or
  when its session breaks, its command gets SIGINT, and SIGKILL after
  10 seconds.

What winch leaves on the host:
  winch keeps its files in /var/lib/winch/:

    bin/                     the helper
    handlers/<project>       pending handlers
    then/<project>           pending then commands
    checks/<project>         pending health gates and asserts
    certbot-hooks/<project>  pending certbot hooks
    downloads/<project>      checksum, owner, and group of each unpack
    firewall.list            the firewall table after its last load
    rollback/                old files for confirm_access, during a run
    reboot                   the boot ID and the error of a failed
                             reboot, until the next boot
    lock                     the session lock

  The helper deletes each other file in bin/ that is older than 30
  days. Each file in handlers/, then/, checks/, certbot-hooks/, and
  downloads/ starts with its format version. A version newer than this
  winch fails the host: upgrade winch to read <file>.

  To remove winch from a host, delete /var/lib/winch. Pending
  handlers, then commands, health gates, asserts, and certbot hooks
  are then lost. The next run of winch starts from the state of the
  host.
